1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
This agent sorts my email. Watch.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Beautiful. Now watch me feed it one weird message.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,319
There it is. The demo you'd post, and the thing you'd never show.

4
00:00:28,319 --> 00:00:36,000
Everyone shows the win. We break the agent on camera, then make it bulletproof.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,192
I'll attack this email assistant twenty-five ways — bad inputs, dead connections, a

6
00:00:40,192 --> 00:00:42,263
lying model, a runaway loop, leaked keys.

7
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:46,505
Every break gets a number, a fix, and a rule, and all twenty-five rules are the free

8
00:00:46,505 --> 00:00:47,566
checklist at the end.

9
00:00:47,566 --> 00:00:51,000
Keep score with me — the counter in the corner ticks once per break.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,789
Quick reason this matters. An agent isn't a chatbot you watch.

11
00:00:55,789 --> 00:01:00,963
It runs on its own, and it acts — it sends, it charges, it deletes.

12
00:01:00,963 --> 00:01:04,362
A confused chatbot just says something dumb.

13
00:01:04,362 --> 00:01:10,000
A confused agent does something dumb, two hundred times, while you sleep.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
And every action can cost.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,231
Each run spends tokens — the chunks of text the model reads and writes, which you

16
00:01:18,231 --> 00:01:18,846
pay for.

17
00:01:18,846 --> 00:01:24,000
Treat them like cash out of a wallet, because that's what they are.

18
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,690
So here's the honest version of building agents. It's not the demo.

19
00:01:28,690 --> 00:01:33,520
It's what happens on day two, when a real inbox throws garbage at it.

20
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:38,000
Let's build the victim, then go break it — all twenty-five ways.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,809
Meet the patient. Fake company: Harbor Supply Co. , a wholesale kitchen outfit.

22
00:01:42,809 --> 00:01:47,922
Their inbox, support@harborsupply. co, gets slammed daily. Our agent's name is Sift.

23
00:01:47,922 --> 00:01:52,000
Everything here is invented — the company, the customers, the keys.

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:53,797
Sift's job sounds simple.

25
00:01:53,797 --> 00:01:59,693
Read each new email, figure out what it is — sales lead, support question, billing

26
00:01:59,693 --> 00:02:05,373
issue, spam, or something urgent — label it, look the person up in our fake CRM

27
00:02:05,373 --> 00:02:11,268
(that's a Customer Relationship Manager, the contact database — ours is Rolodash),

28
00:02:11,268 --> 00:02:14,000
and draft a reply for a human to send.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,240
Here's the first honest call, and it's a big one.

30
00:02:18,240 --> 00:02:23,000
Most of that job does not need artificial intelligence.

31
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,238
Break the job into chunks and ask, for each one, does this need judgment?

32
00:02:28,238 --> 00:02:34,265
\"Does this email contain the word invoice? \" — that's not judgment, that's a rule.

33
00:02:34,265 --> 00:02:40,004
\"Is this the fourth email from this person today? \" — a counter, not a genius.

34
00:02:40,004 --> 00:02:45,960
The only chunk that truly needs a model is reading a messy, human-written email and

35
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:47,753
deciding what it's about.

36
00:02:47,753 --> 00:02:53,637
So we hard-code the boring chunks and reserve the model for the one that needs it.

37
00:02:53,637 --> 00:02:55,000
Boring is reliable.

38
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:01,072
That one reasoning chunk needs a system prompt — the standing instructions that tell

39
00:03:01,072 --> 00:03:03,819
the model who it is and how to behave.

40
00:03:03,819 --> 00:03:05,916
We'll build that in a second.

41
00:03:05,916 --> 00:03:09,964
First, let's give Sift no help at all and watch it fail.

42
00:03:09,964 --> 00:03:13,000
That's Round One — breaks one through six.

43
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,710
Break number one: the empty brain.

44
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:20,014
I wired Sift up with tools but almost no instructions.

45
00:03:20,014 --> 00:03:24,000
Watch what a vague agent does with a normal email.

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,978
Generic mush. That's the classic signature of a missing system prompt.

47
00:03:28,978 --> 00:03:32,462
When an agent has no role, it writes like nobody.

48
00:03:32,462 --> 00:03:37,369
No defined context, it gets confused about what it's even looking at.

49
00:03:37,369 --> 00:03:40,000
No rules, it starts making things up.

50
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,115
So let's give it a real prompt with the sections that matter.

51
00:03:44,115 --> 00:03:46,813
A background — who Sift is and its goal.

52
00:03:46,813 --> 00:03:52,075
A context section — what it receives each run, since the email changes but the

53
00:03:52,075 --> 00:03:53,357
instructions don't.

54
00:03:53,357 --> 00:03:59,024
A tools section — each tool, when to use it, and the order, like \"look the customer

55
00:03:59,024 --> 00:04:01,183
up in Rolodash before you draft.

56
00:04:01,183 --> 00:04:06,040
\" Rules as conditions — if the email mentions a refund, tag it billing.

57
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:08,940
Examples, but only for stuff it gets wrong.

58
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:14,000
And final notes — today's date, and a plain \"if you don't know, say so. \"

59
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,098
Notice what I did not do.

60
00:04:16,098 --> 00:04:22,476
I did not paste in a giant three-page prompt I generated somewhere and hope.

61
00:04:22,476 --> 00:04:26,000
I built it reactively — one fix at a time.

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,221
Reactive prompting is like seasoning a pot of soup.

63
00:04:30,221 --> 00:04:34,195
You don't dump in every spice up front and hope.

64
00:04:34,195 --> 00:04:40,695
You taste it, add one pinch, taste again — because if you throw five things in at

65
00:04:40,695 --> 00:04:46,075
once and it comes out wrong, you have no idea which one to blame.

66
00:04:46,075 --> 00:04:52,575
Same here: add one tool, test a real email, watch what Sift does, then add one line

67
00:04:52,575 --> 00:04:55,886
to correct the exact thing it got wrong.

68
00:04:55,886 --> 00:05:03,000
Change one thing at a time so you always know which line caused which behavior.

69
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:07,077
And when it does get something wrong, hard-prompt it.

70
00:05:07,077 --> 00:05:13,308
Paste the real email it fumbled, the wrong move it made, and the move you wanted.

71
00:05:13,308 --> 00:05:14,615
Show it the miss.

72
00:05:14,615 --> 00:05:20,615
Don't waste examples on emails it already handles fine — those are just tokens

73
00:05:20,615 --> 00:05:22,000
you're paying for.

74
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,754
Now for the ugly input.

75
00:05:23,754 --> 00:05:30,008
Real inboxes are full of garbage, so I sent Sift a malformed one — a customer form

76
00:05:30,008 --> 00:05:33,212
that dumped raw broken data into the body.

77
00:05:33,212 --> 00:05:39,619
Sift passed that mess straight into the Rolodash lookup, and the connection screamed

78
00:05:39,619 --> 00:05:40,000
back.

79
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:41,864
Time to read status codes.

80
00:05:41,864 --> 00:05:47,885
Every time an agent talks to another service over the web, that service answers with

81
00:05:47,885 --> 00:05:53,835
an HTTP status code — HTTP is just the language web services speak, and the code is

82
00:05:53,835 --> 00:05:56,631
a three-digit verdict on what happened.

83
00:05:56,631 --> 00:06:00,000
Learn the families and you save yourself hours.

84
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,046
Two hundreds mean success.

85
00:06:02,046 --> 00:06:08,343
Four hundreds mean you messed up the request — a four-oh-oh is usually malformed

86
00:06:08,343 --> 00:06:14,718
data like a stray comma or a broken field, exactly what our garbage input caused.

87
00:06:14,718 --> 00:06:21,218
Four-oh-one means your key is wrong. Four-oh-three means your account isn't allowed.

88
00:06:21,218 --> 00:06:24,444
Four-oh-four means the address is a typo.

89
00:06:24,444 --> 00:06:30,944
And five hundreds mean the other server broke — not your fault, so stop rewriting a

90
00:06:30,944 --> 00:06:34,000
good request and just wait and retry.

91
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:40,176
The fix for our four-oh-oh: validate and clean the input before it reaches a tool.

92
00:06:40,176 --> 00:06:45,824
Expect garbage, strip it, and confirm required fields exist before you call

93
00:06:45,824 --> 00:06:46,502
anything.

94
00:06:46,502 --> 00:06:52,000
Rule for the checklist: check your inputs before your agent acts on them.

95
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:52,722
Round two.

96
00:06:52,722 --> 00:06:58,500
Things break that aren't your fault, and the worst part is you often won't know.

97
00:06:58,500 --> 00:07:04,278
Watch this — I quietly expire Sift's Rolodash key, like a credential that lapsed

98
00:07:04,278 --> 00:07:05,000
overnight.

99
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,189
See the trap? It looks fine.

100
00:07:07,189 --> 00:07:13,601
But a run can go green while a tool inside it quietly failed — a call errored, and

101
00:07:13,601 --> 00:07:16,103
the agent shrugged and moved on.

102
00:07:16,103 --> 00:07:18,918
Green is not proof the thing worked.

103
00:07:18,918 --> 00:07:24,000
That's how agents fail silently for a week before anyone notices.

104
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,497
So we build one error-logging workflow, once, and reuse it everywhere.

105
00:07:28,497 --> 00:07:33,830
It starts with an error trigger, and every other workflow points its \"on failure\"

106
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:34,729
setting at it.

107
00:07:34,729 --> 00:07:39,547
When something goes red, the handler catches the details — which run, which

108
00:07:39,547 --> 00:07:44,944
workflow, which step failed, and the error message — logs a row, and pings me with a

109
00:07:44,944 --> 00:07:47,000
link straight to the broken run.

110
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:51,483
To prove it works, I forced a failure and watched the alert land.

111
00:07:51,483 --> 00:07:55,828
One honest caveat: this only catches runs that actually go red.

112
00:07:55,828 --> 00:08:01,483
A tool that fails quietly while the step stays green still slips through — so pair

113
00:08:01,483 --> 00:08:05,000
the log with a real check that the action happened.

114
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,833
Now the fragile chain.

115
00:08:06,833 --> 00:08:13,333
Sift's draft-reply step leans on an outside writing service that gets flaky under

116
00:08:13,333 --> 00:08:13,933
load.

117
00:08:13,933 --> 00:08:20,017
When it hiccups, the old Sift returned nothing and the whole run stalled.

118
00:08:20,017 --> 00:08:26,000
So we split that step into two exits: a success path and an error path.

119
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,557
On success, it returns the finished draft, normal as ever.

120
00:08:29,557 --> 00:08:34,524
On error, instead of dying, it returns a short, plain-English message: \"couldn't

121
00:08:34,524 --> 00:08:36,547
draft that one, please try again.

122
00:08:36,547 --> 00:08:41,269
\" The main agent reads that sentence like a note from a coworker, and simply

123
00:08:41,269 --> 00:08:42,373
reissues the task.

124
00:08:42,373 --> 00:08:45,684
A flaky failure becomes a retry instead of a dead run.

125
00:08:45,684 --> 00:08:50,712
Keep that failure message human-readable — the next step reads it as instructions,

126
00:08:50,712 --> 00:08:52,000
not as a stack trace.

127
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,029
Quick note.

128
00:08:53,029 --> 00:08:59,529
Every rule we've earned is landing on one page as we go — the Agent Pre-Launch

129
00:08:59,529 --> 00:09:02,615
Checklist, yours free at the end.

130
00:09:02,615 --> 00:09:05,000
Watch it fill up.

131
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,474
Round three, and this is the sneaky one. The model doesn't crash.

132
00:09:10,474 --> 00:09:13,000
It lies with total confidence.

133
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,881
Sounds perfect. It's fiction. There is no GreyRoute, there is no tracking number.

134
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:24,908
Sift invented a shipping update because it sounded like the kind of thing that goes

135
00:09:24,908 --> 00:09:26,142
in that sentence.

136
00:09:26,142 --> 00:09:32,241
This is a hallucination — a made-up fact stated as truth — and if a human hits send,

137
00:09:32,241 --> 00:09:35,000
we just lied to a customer in writing.

138
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:42,000
The fix isn't to yell at the model. It's a mindset: every failure is data.

139
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,530
So we diagnose it — Sift was drafting shipping details it never looked up.

140
00:09:47,530 --> 00:09:53,433
We fix the underlying step: force it to pull real order data from the source of

141
00:09:53,433 --> 00:09:57,095
truth first, and forbid any status it can't cite.

142
00:09:57,095 --> 00:09:59,187
We retest on the same email.

143
00:09:59,187 --> 00:10:05,389
Then — the part people skip — we write the lesson into the workflow's own rules, so

144
00:10:05,389 --> 00:10:11,591
this exact failure can't come back, and save it to a memory file so it survives the

145
00:10:11,591 --> 00:10:13,982
next run, not just this session.

146
00:10:13,982 --> 00:10:16,000
Fix once, remember forever.

147
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:22,500
Round four. Autonomous agents are eager — give one no limits and it will act, a lot.

148
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:27,799
Remember the hook, where Sift emailed the same customer four times?

149
00:10:27,799 --> 00:10:30,000
Here's why, at full scale.

150
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,727
An eager agent with no guardrails is a puppy with the keys to the warehouse — not

151
00:10:35,727 --> 00:10:40,818
malicious, just enthusiastic, and enthusiasm at machine speed is a bill.

152
00:10:40,818 --> 00:10:44,000
So we bound the blast radius with hard rules.

153
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:49,950
First, start in paper mode — a safe simulation where Sift drafts and logs what it

154
00:10:49,950 --> 00:10:53,844
would send, but sends nothing real, until I trust it.

155
00:10:53,844 --> 00:11:00,014
Then write explicit caps and prohibitions into its rules: one reply per customer per

156
00:11:00,014 --> 00:11:05,818
run, a hard limit per hour, and never touch anything outside the support inbox.

157
00:11:05,818 --> 00:11:11,915
And early on, I read the full history of its runs and tune from what I see, instead

158
00:11:11,915 --> 00:11:15,000
of auto-accepting whatever it wants to do.

159
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,921
Second half of this round is quieter but just as expensive: context.

160
00:11:19,921 --> 00:11:25,711
Sift was re-reading the customer's entire two-year email history on every single

161
00:11:25,711 --> 00:11:26,000
run.

162
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,393
Two problems. One, you pay for every token, every run — cash on fire.

163
00:11:31,393 --> 00:11:36,864
Two, and this surprises people, more context can make the agent worse.

164
00:11:36,864 --> 00:11:42,726
Stuff too much in and quality rots — the model loses the plot in the noise.

165
00:11:42,726 --> 00:11:49,136
So budget context like money: give each run only what it needs — the instructions,

166
00:11:49,136 --> 00:11:54,451
the current email, a short customer summary — not the whole archive.

167
00:11:54,451 --> 00:12:00,000
Keep memory files short. A lean agent is a sharp agent and a cheap one.

168
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Round five, the one that ends careers. Secrets and permissions.

169
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:11,854
Break number one here: I found the Rolodash key pasted directly into the agent's

170
00:12:11,854 --> 00:12:13,902
instructions, in plain text.

171
00:12:13,902 --> 00:12:18,000
Something like rd_live_8fQ2x… right there in the prompt.

172
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,141
That's a leak waiting to happen.

173
00:12:20,141 --> 00:12:25,693
A key in your prompt or code can ride along into a repository, a screenshot, or the

174
00:12:25,693 --> 00:12:30,175
chat history — and chat history can travel off to a model provider.

175
00:12:30,175 --> 00:12:31,111
The fix is a .

176
00:12:31,111 --> 00:12:36,597
env file: a plain settings file, kept out of version control, that holds your keys

177
00:12:36,597 --> 00:12:38,202
under placeholder names.

178
00:12:38,202 --> 00:12:41,748
Your code refers to the key by name, never its value.

179
00:12:41,748 --> 00:12:47,099
You paste the real value into that file yourself, locally — never hand it to the

180
00:12:47,099 --> 00:12:52,184
agent in chat — and add the file to your ignore list so it can't get pushed.

181
00:12:52,184 --> 00:12:57,000
If a key ever leaks, you rotate it: cancel the old one, issue a new one.

182
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,233
Break number two: over-privilege.

183
00:12:59,233 --> 00:13:04,850
Sift was logged into my own account, with full access to everything — send, delete,

184
00:13:04,850 --> 00:13:06,000
admin, the works.

185
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:07,789
Think of it like a valet key.

186
00:13:07,789 --> 00:13:12,973
You hand the parking attendant a key that starts the car and nothing else — it won't

187
00:13:12,973 --> 00:13:16,243
open the trunk or the glovebox where your things are.

188
00:13:16,243 --> 00:13:21,426
Sift gets the same treatment: its own dedicated account, scoped to the support inbox

189
00:13:21,426 --> 00:13:26,301
and read-only on the CRM, since it only needs to look people up, not edit them.

190
00:13:26,301 --> 00:13:31,361
And its own named key — so when I check the bill I can see exactly what Sift spent

191
00:13:31,361 --> 00:13:33,768
and did, separate from everything else.

192
00:13:33,768 --> 00:13:38,889
Least privilege shrinks the damage any single agent can do, and makes its costs and

193
00:13:38,889 --> 00:13:40,000
actions traceable.

194
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,222
One net under all of it.

195
00:13:42,222 --> 00:13:48,722
Before Sift sends anything a customer will actually read, it stops and asks a

196
00:13:48,722 --> 00:13:50,000
person.

197
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,023
The pattern is send-and-wait.

198
00:13:52,023 --> 00:13:57,465
Sift drafts the reply, pauses, and pushes it to me — in our case a quick Slack

199
00:13:57,465 --> 00:14:00,465
message — and does nothing until I respond.

200
00:14:00,465 --> 00:14:06,256
I can approve it, or just type back what I want changed in plain English: \"warmer,

201
00:14:06,256 --> 00:14:07,860
and mention the reship.

202
00:14:07,860 --> 00:14:08,000
\"

203
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,774
Here's the clever part. A yes-or-no is easy, but free-text feedback isn't.

204
00:14:13,774 --> 00:14:20,251
So a small AI classifier reads my reply and decides: approval, or revision request?

205
00:14:20,251 --> 00:14:23,216
Rules alone couldn't tell those apart.

206
00:14:23,216 --> 00:14:29,458
If it's a revision, my feedback and the current draft go to a revision step that

207
00:14:29,458 --> 00:14:32,657
rewrites it, then loops right back to me.

208
00:14:32,657 --> 00:14:38,977
It can keep stacking edit on edit, round after round, until I sign off — and each

209
00:14:38,977 --> 00:14:45,298
pass has to build on the newest version, never the first draft, or the fixes just

210
00:14:45,298 --> 00:14:46,000
get lost.

211
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:52,500
So let's run the whole gauntlet again, on the hardened Sift, and see what's left

212
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:54,000
standing.

213
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:59,891
The malformed input? Cleaned before the call — no more four-oh-oh. The expired key?

214
00:14:59,891 --> 00:15:04,647
The error handler catches it and pings me. The flaky draft service?

215
00:15:04,647 --> 00:15:08,551
Retries and recovers. The hallucinated tracking number?

216
00:15:08,551 --> 00:15:14,514
Gone — Sift now says \"let me check\" instead of inventing a fact. The runaway loop?

217
00:15:14,514 --> 00:15:20,405
Capped at one reply per customer, in paper mode until I trusted it. The leaked key?

218
00:15:20,405 --> 00:15:24,096
In an ignored . env file, on its own scoped account.

219
00:15:24,096 --> 00:15:28,000
And nothing customer-facing ships without my thumbs-up.

220
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,160
Same agent, same inbox.

221
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:36,660
The difference is every one of those failures now has a floor under it.

222
00:15:36,660 --> 00:15:43,160
That's the whole game — not a flashier demo, a system that doesn't fall over on day

223
00:15:43,160 --> 00:15:45,000
two.

224
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,163
Every rule we just earned is on one page.

225
00:15:48,163 --> 00:15:54,180
It's called the Agent Pre-Launch Checklist — about twenty-five checks, grouped

226
00:15:54,180 --> 00:16:00,120
exactly like these rounds: input and scope, errors and retries, verification,

227
00:16:00,120 --> 00:16:04,903
guardrails and cost, secrets and permissions, and human gates.

228
00:16:04,903 --> 00:16:08,606
Run it before you let any agent touch real work.

229
00:16:08,606 --> 00:16:12,000
Free link in the description, no email wall.

230
